          }
        ],
        "student_prefix_sha256": "eace9c28371bd2c6efbe6380739ddf38110ab1803b990b5a95583bcbaf681a41",
        "target": "Given that the book is 600 pages long, I first calculated the number of pages Coral reads in the first week. Since she reads half of it, I divided 600 by 2, which gave me 300 pages. This means she has 600 - 300 = 300 pages left to read after the first week. Next, I determined how many pages she reads in the second week, which is 30% of the remaining 300 pages. I calculated 300 * 0.30 = 90 pages. After the second week, she has 300 - 90 = 210 pages left to read. To finish the book, she must read 210 pages in the third week.\n\nTo verify this, I checked the total pages read over the three weeks: 300 (first week) + 90 (second week) + 210 (third week) = 600 pages, which matches the total number of pages in the book. This confirms that the solution is correct.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is calculating the remaining pages after a certain number of weeks and then determining the number of pages to read in the next week based on a percentage of the remaining pages. This is useful for similar problems involving reading progress over multiple weeks.\n\nFINAL: 210",
